@local/dsh-turn-recap README · Requirements：The same grant is available in Plugin Manager. It authorizes in-process code against a runtime it was never verified
with — crashes and data loss are the documented risk — and it applies to one exact plugin version on one exact runtime
version.

@local/dsh-turn-recap README · Install：This package is a DSH bundle: it declares `dsh.bundle.patch` and a `dsh.client` half, so it installs into a profile with
the launcher's own plugin command. Nothing is published to npm.

```bash

@local/dsh-turn-recap README · How it works：**Why a projection instead of a session event?** A plugin may not append new event types: a log carrying a type the
build does not know cannot be reopened, and `appendPluginRecord()` is reserved for first-party experimental packages.
The session projection is the supported carrier for session-derived client state.

@local/dsh-turn-recap README · How it works：**`/recap`** is a plain DSH command: it runs outside any turn, and its result travels in `command/done`. The Client half
claims the command row through the `conversation.chat.commandview` slot under the `recap` key and renders the running,
success, and failure states itself.

